          %h2#idea Idea
          %p A bimonoid is something which is both a monoid and a comonoid in a compatible way. The compatibility is easy to formulate in symmetric monoidal categories and much harder in a nonsymmetric setup.
          %h2#definition_in_symmetric_monoidal_categories Definition in symmetric monoidal categories
          %p
            In a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+monoidal+category"} symmetric monoidal category
            %strong bimonoid
            (or
            = succeed ")" do
              %strong bimonoid object
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            equipped with a structure of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoid"} monoid
            and a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/comonoid"} comonoid
            which are compatible in one of two equivalent ways: the comultiplication and the counit are morphisms of monoids or the multiplication and the unit are morphisms of comonoids. The symmetry of the monoidal structure is involved in the definition of the tensor product
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B\otimes B
            as monoids and as comonoids. In terms of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+diagrams"} string diagrams
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            may be represented as follows:
          %center
            %img{:align => "middle", :height => "100", :src => "/nlab/files/bimonoid-eq1.png"}/
                  
            %img{:align => "middle", :height => "60", :src => "/nlab/files/bimonoid-eq2.png"}/
                  
            %img{:align => "middle", :height => "60", :src => "/nlab/files/bimonoid-eq3.png"}/
                  
            %img{:align => "middle", :height => "50", :src => "/nlab/files/bimonoid-eq4.png"}/
          %p
            A bimonoid that additionally has an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/antipode"} antipode
            (a map
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi s
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi B
                  %mo →
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s \colon B\to B
            satisfying an axiom that makes it act like “inverses” in a group) is called a
            = succeed ";" do
              %strong
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hopf+monoid"} Hopf monoid
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Vect"} Vect
            is a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hopf+algebra"} Hopf algebra
          %p
            A bimonoid that is both a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+monoid"} commutative monoid
            and a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cocommutative+comonoid"} cocommutative comonoid
            is called
            = succeed "." do
              %strong bicommutative
          %h2#examples Examples
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                A bimonoid in
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Vect"} Vect
                (with its usual
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product"} tensor product
                = succeed "." do
                  %strong
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bialgebra"} bialgebra
            %li
              %p
                In a category with
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/biproducts"} biproducts
                = succeed ")." do
                  %a{:href => "http://golem.ph.utexas.edu/category/2010/09/bimonoids_from_biproducts.html"} Café post
            %li
              %p
                More generally, in a
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+monoidal+category"} cartesian monoidal category
            %li
              %p
                A bimonoid is said to be
                %strong special
                if comultiplication followed by multiplication is the identity. The category
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Rel"} Rel
                has biproducts so every object is a bicommutative bimonoid, but these bimonoids are also special. The category
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/FinRel"} FinRel
                is the free symmetric monoidal category on a special bicommutative bimonoid, namely
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
                with its diagonal as comultiplication and fold map as multiplication.
            %li
              %p
                A bimonoid is said to be
                %strong connected
                if counit followed by unit is the identity. For example, the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+algebra"} symmetric algebra
                is a connected bimonoid.
            %li
              %p
                One obtains the notion of
                %strong quasi-bimonoid
                by omitting the associativity and coassociativity requirements.
          %h2#MonoidalStructureOnModules Monoidal structure on modules
          %p
            If
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            is a bimonoid in
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Mod
                    %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Mod_B
            of
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modules"} modules
            inherits a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+category"} monoidal structure
            such that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/forgetful+functor"} forgetful functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Mod
                    %mi B
                  %mo →
                  %mi 𝒞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Mod_B \to \mathcal{C}
            (the “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fiber+functor"}> fiber functor
            ”) is a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strong+monoidal+functor"} strong monoidal functor
            = succeed "-modules" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi N
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product"} tensor product
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi N
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M\otimes N
            in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
            with the
            = succeed "-action" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi M
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi N
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mi Δ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi B
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi M
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi N
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mo ≅
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi B
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi M
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi B
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi N
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mrow
                      %mi act
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mi act
                  %mi M
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi N
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  B\otimes (M\otimes N) \xrightarrow{\Delta}
                  (B\otimes B) \otimes (M\otimes N) \xrightarrow{\cong}
                  (B\otimes M) \otimes (B\otimes N) \xrightarrow{act \otimes act}
                  M\otimes N
          %p
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Δ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta
            denotes the comultiplication of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            as a comonoid. The bimonoid compatibility axioms are exactly what is needed to make this a
            = succeed "-module" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                  %mo →
                  %mi I
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B\to I
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            to give the unit object
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi I
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I
            a
            = succeed "-module" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
          %p
            If
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            moreover a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hopf+monoid"} Hopf monoid
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Mod
                    %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Mod_B
            also inherits a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+monoidal+category"} closed
            monoidal structure if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
            has one; see at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hopf+monoid"} Hopf monoid
          %p
            These relations are known as
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tannaka+duality"} Tannaka duality
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "Tannaka%20duality#ForAlgebraModules"} for monoids/algebras
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structure+on+algebras+and+their+module+categories+-+table"} structure on algebras and their module categories - table
          %h2#nonsymmetric_variants Non-symmetric variants
          %p
            It is interesting how to generalize this notion in various nonsymmetric situations, for example involving
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/braided+monoidal+category"} braidings
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/duoidal+categories"} duoidal categories
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monad"} monad
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mixed+distributive+laws"} mixed distributive laws
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hopf+monoid"}> Hopf monoid
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Frobenius+monoid"} Frobenius monoid
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/graded+bimonoid"} graded bimonoid
          %h2#references References
          %p
            As far as compatibility with tensor product is concerned, there is a notion of a bimonad and involves a version of
            = succeed "s," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/distributive+law"} distributive law
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ieke+Moerdijk"}> Ieke Moerdijk
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Monads on tensor categories
            %li
              %p
                Kornél Szlachányi,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em The monoidal Eilenberg–Moore construction and bialgebroids
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Adjointable monoidal functors and quantum groupoids
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/math/0301253"} math.QA/0301253
          %p Szlachányi uses earlier analysis of
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+Schauenburg"}> Peter Schauenburg
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Bialgebras over noncommutative rings, and a structure theorem for Hopf bimodules
                = succeed "," do
                  %strong 6
                %a{:href => "http://dx.doi.org/10.1023/A:1008608028634"} doi
            %li
              %p
                H.E. Porst,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em On categories of monoids, comonoids and bimonoids
                %strong 31
                (2008) 127-139
                %a{:href => "http://www.ams.org/mathscinet-getitem?mr=2529129"} MR2010d:18010
                %a{:href => "http://dx.doi.org/10.2989/QM.2008.31.2.2.474"} doi
          %p For a dual version see
          %ul
            %li
              P. Mac Crudden, Opmonoidal monads, Theory Appl. Cat., Vol. 10, 2002, No. 19, pp 469-485,
              %a{:href => "http://www.tac.mta.ca/tac/volumes/10/19/10-19abs.html"} link
          %p There is also a more subtle notion also called Hopf monad in
          %ul
            %li
              R. Wisbauer, B. Mesablishvili, Bimonads and Hopf monads on categories,
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/0710.1163"} arXiv:0710.1163
          %p
            In some of these generalized cases, one does not have a good notion of of antipode, so that the difference between bimonoids and Hopf monoids has to be stated in different terms. A similar case is in the case of
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hopf+algebroid"} Hopf algebroid
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Brian+Day"}> Brian Day
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ross+Street"} Ross Street
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Monoidal bicategories and Hopf algebroids
                = succeed "," do
                  %strong 129
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gabi+B%C3%B6hm"}> Gabi Böhm
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Hopf algebroids
                = succeed ";" do
                  %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/0805.3806"} arxiv:math.RA/0805.3806
                = succeed ";" do
                  %em An alternative notion of Hopf algebroid
                = succeed "," do
                  %strong 239
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/math.QA/0301169"} math.QA/0301169
          %p
            The fact that a skeleton of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/FinRel"} FinRel
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/PROP"} PROP
            for special bicommutative bimonoids is Theorem 7.2 in Coya and Fong, based on techniques from Lack’s paper on distributive laws for PROPs:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Brandon Coya and
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Brendan+Fong"} Brendan Fong
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Corelations are the prop for extraspecial commutative Frobenius monoids
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1601.02307"} arxiv
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Steve+Lack"}> Steve Lack
                , Composing PROPs, Theory and Applications of Categories 13(9):147–163, 2004. (
                %a{:href => "http://www.tac.mta.ca/tac/volumes/13/9/13-09.pdf"}> pdf
                )
